<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietro II Orseolo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/961">961</a>–<a href="/years/1009">1009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietro II Orseolo was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a> from 991 to 1009. He secured trading privileges from the Byzantine emperor and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and in 1000 led a fleet along the Dalmatian coast that broke the power of the Narentine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and brought the coastal towns under Venetian protection, taking the title Duke of the Dalmatians. The expedition was commemorated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marriage-of-the-sea-ceremony">Marriage of the Sea ceremony</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietro-ii-orseolo.jpg" alt="Pietro II Orseolo" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maspez · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Orseolo_II_Palazzo_Ducale_Venezia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pietro II Orseolo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 961, the only son of Doge Pietro I Orseolo and Felicia Malipiero. His father became doge in 976 but retired after two years to the Abbey of Saint-Michel-de-Cuxa. Pietro was elected doge in 991, succeeding Tribuno Memmo, who had been forced to abdicate amid violent clashes between the Coloprini and Morosini families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Pietro began in favourable circumstances. He agreed with the Byzantine emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> and Constantine VIII to carry Byzantine troops, and received a golden bull giving Venetian merchants wide privileges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Emperor Otto III confirmed similar rights in a diploma of 19 July 992.</p><p>After the Dalmatian city-states complained in 997, the Venetian fleet attacked the Neretvian pirates, or Narentines, on Ascension Day in 998, and Pietro took the title Duke of the Dalmatians. On 9 May 1000 he set out to pacify the Croatians and the Narentines. His fleet scorched the eastern Adriatic coast and caught the Neretvians near the island of Kača after they had captured forty traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>. At Split Pietro agreed to release the prisoners if their leader bowed before him, they gave up the tax <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had paid since 948, and Venetian ships had safe passage. He kept six hostages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korcula">Korčula</a> resisted and was conquered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lastovo">Lastovo</a>, known as a pirate haven, was evacuated and razed.</p><p>In 1002 he named his son Giovanni co-doge. That year or the next he led a hundred ships to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, besieged by Saracens, with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>. Giovanni married Maria Argyra, a cousin of the emperor, but in 1007 Giovanni, Maria and their son Basil died of plague. Pietro made his son Otto co-doge and died soon afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Pietro began the eastward expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which lasted for much of five centuries, and his actions mark the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Mar</a>, the overseas dominion of Venice. He is sometimes called the Great Doge. The date of his victory became that of the Festa della Sensa, the Ascension festival and the oldest in Venice, on which the doge and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Olivolo blessed the waters past the Lido.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietro II Orseolo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1000 Doge <b>Pietro II Orseolo</b> led an expedition that brought the Dalmatian coast under Venetian protection, an event commemorated in the annual ceremony of the marriage of the sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1000 Doge <b>Pietro II Orseolo</b> led a fleet that broke Narentine sea power.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagania">Pagania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=992" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pietro II Orseolo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=992">What was here in 992 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
